# On The Gravitational Nature Of Time

<Hint label="Update"> This is a summary of the more academic article available [here](/paper)</Hint>

<Admonition title="What is this?">

This is both a sample note representing what Conundrum is capable of and a short summary of a modified model of relativity that inspired Conundrum and Fluster; let me explain...

It was early 2021 when I was putting aside my software work for the night at 3am. I had put in some toast when I opened a book I had sitting there, a book of Einstein's lectures. I flipped to a random page when something stood out at me: Einstein's famous quote on the length of moving rods from "On The Electrodynamics of Moving Bodies".

Maybe it was just seeing that line out of context that triggered something in me, but I realized that Einstein's insistence on a static (non-expanding) Universe early in his career led him to make certain assumptions that I feel should be revisited in the context of cosmic inflation.

I quit my job as a software engineer to work on this model full time, studying physics while living on the side of the street with my dog but I think I have it: Classically synchronous General Relativity.

<Hint>
There are two video walkthroughs on our [Youtube](https://www.youtube.com/@Fluster_io) channel, as well as an `.ipynb` [notebook](https://github.com/flusterIO/alphaOmegaGravity/blob/main/alpha_omega_gravity.ipynb), a python package `alpha_omega_gravity`, and a standalone mac application to explore the model further. Those accompany the earlier version of this note.
</Hint>

</Admonition>

<Card title="Where things stand">

I'll be upfront about this as we go, but here's the scorecard.

**Solid (given the vacuum equations of GR):** the Lorentz factor as the slope of a fourth spatial coordinate, the geometry of Schwarzschild and of the whole expanding Universe from that one slope, gravitational time dilation falling out of the same picture, and a neat link between a falling apple and cosmic expansion.

**A hypothesis (with predictions I can be proven wrong by):** inertia is Machian and switches regime at an acceleration around $cH_0$. I test it against the Milky Way below.

**Number fitting:** a handful of coincidences I find fun and can't justify. They're clearly labeled.
</Card>
